From d697405d3b97c2f044244751a77e5fca3de7a9d3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: My Naai <dev@mynaai.in>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 10:17:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Push: stop burying the credential failure, and make the key
 an environment concern
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Production logs, every 20 seconds, once per device token:

  Error sending push notification: FirebaseAppError: … invalid_grant: Invalid JWT
  Signature … (1) your server time is not properly synced or (2) your certificate key
  file has been revoked
  🚀 FCM PAYLOAD SENT: { … "token": "f6f8vidlSvybx0_jowcc-x:APA91b…" }

Three things wrong with that, in the order they cost the most:

**The key was in the repository.** `src/firebase/myNaai-firebase.json` is a full
service account — private_key and private_key_id — committed to a public repo.
Google revokes service-account keys it finds in public code, and a revoked key
produces exactly "Invalid JWT Signature". No code fix can restore it; the key has
to be generated again in the console. What the code can do is stop needing a key
file in the repo at all, and say which of Google's two causes actually applies.

**Nothing said which cause it was.** "Server time or revoked key" is a guess.
`src/firebase/credentialsDoctor.js` now answers it: it signs the exact JWT
firebase-admin would send and verifies it against the certificate Google
publishes for that private_key_id (so a deleted key and a mismatched private_key
are different verdicts), and it compares the local clock with Google's. Verdicts:
credentials-look-valid / key-revoked / key-mismatch / clock-skew /
clock-skew-and-key-revoked / cannot-check-online.

**The failure path was dangerous and noisy.** "PAYLOAD SENT" was printed before
the send, so a failed push logged as a sent one, with the token in full. Every
failure printed a stack trace per token per sweep. And a credential failure ran
through the token-cleanup branch — one widened condition away from deactivating
every live device token during an outage.

Now:

* `src/firebase/credentials.js` reads the key from the environment first
  (FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON → _PATH → GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS →
  the old FIREBASE_* trio → the committed file, deprecated), repairs the ways a
  key arrives broken (\n, double-escaped \\n, quotes, BOM, re-wrapped body), and
  validates it before firebase-admin sees it. A configured-but-broken variable is
  reported, not silently skipped.
* `firebase.js` no longer throws at require time: a bad credential logs one
  actionable block and everything except push keeps working.
* `sendNotification.js`: "sent" only after the send resolved; tokens masked; the
  payload only under FIREBASE_PUSH_DEBUG=1; a credential failure suspends push
  for 10 minutes with ONE diagnosis; a device token is deactivated only for a
  genuinely dead token; transient errors get one retry.
* `cron/bookingRequestSweeper.cron.js` stops querying salon tokens while push is
  suspended, and keeps writing the in-app notification either way.
* `npm run firebase:check [-- --online | -- --send <token>]` answers "which cause
  is it, on this server?" in one command.

Tests: `node scripts/test-firebase-credentials.js` → 48 checks, all offline (a
throwaway RSA keypair and stubbed firebase/db), including the production error
verbatim: it must not throw, must not deactivate the token, and must suspend push.

Docs: docs/FIREBASE-PUSH-CREDENTIALS.md in the web repo.
